<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tyburn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tyburn was a village west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, near today's Marble Arch, whose name became a byword for execution. From 1196 to 1783 it was the main place of public hanging for London and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, from 1571 using a large triangular gallows known as the Tyburn Tree. Among those executed there were Catholic martyrs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era and the Irish lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-orourke">Brian O'Rourke</a> in 1591.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyburn.jpg" alt="Tyburn" width="250" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Rocque The original uploader was ChrisO~enwiki at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyburn_gallows_1746.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tyburn was a manor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, one of two served by the parish of Marylebone, and took its name from the Tyburn Brook, a tributary of the River Westbourne; Teo Bourne means boundary stream. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 records it with eight households, held before and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> by the nuns of Barking Abbey. It was bounded by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>, the modern Edgware Road and Oxford Street, whose junction was marked by Oswulf's Stone, which gave its name to the Ossulstone Hundred. In 1236 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> contracted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> de Sandford to draw water from the Tyburn Springs for its first piped supply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded execution took place in 1196, when William Fitz Osbert, leader of a popular revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, was dragged behind a horse to Tyburn and hanged. Executions for London and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a> were moved there from Smithfield in the 1400s, and in 1537 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> hanged leaders of the Pilgrimage of Grace there. The Tyburn Tree, a wooden triangle on three legs, was put up in 1571, and its first victim was the Catholic John Story on 1 June 1571. On 23 June 1649 it hanged 24 prisoners at once, and in January 1661 the bodies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, Henry Ireton and John Bradshaw were hanged on it on the orders of the Cavalier Parliament.</p><p>The condemned rode from Newgate in an open cart, about three miles that could take three hours, stopping for a last drink at the Bowl in St Giles. Crowds of thousands watched, the wealthy from paid stands, with traders, pickpockets and surgeons seeking bodies for dissection. A ride to Tyburn meant going to be hanged. Movable gallows replaced the fixed ones in 1759, and the last hanging, of the highwayman John Austin, took place on 3 November 1783; executions then moved to Newgate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site of the Tyburn Tree lies near Marble Arch, where three oak trees planted in 2014 on a traffic island at the junction of Edgware Road and Bayswater Road surround a roundel inscribed The site of Tyburn Tree. The Tyburn Convent remembers the Catholic martyrs executed there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tyburn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brian O'Rourke sheltered Spanish Armada survivors in 1588 and was executed at <b>Tyburn</b> in 1591 for treason.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-breifne">West Breifne</a></li></ul></section>
